      "textEn": "37\n\nWhen the Bhrigu dynasty priest and his disciples were engaged in various arguments, at that\ntime, Lord Vamandev, holding a staff, an umbrella, and a water-filled kamandal, entered the Ashwamedha\nYagya hall. Maujya Mekhala Veetamupavitrajintottaram. Complex Vamana, Brahma, Maya, Vamana, Hari. 24). The one who entered, seeing the Bhrigu, was burned by the fire. By the power of his brilliance, he was captured. 25. Meaning of the word:\n\nMaujya—made of mud; Mekhala—from a box; Veetam—circumferential; Upaveet—the sacred thread; Ajin-Uttaram—the northern garment of deer skin; Wamanam—to the dwarf; Vipram—the Brahmin; Maya-Manavam—the illusory son of man;\nHarim—to the Lord; Pravishtam—entered; Vikshya—by looking; Bhrigavaah—all the Bhrigu dynasty priests; Sa-Shishyah—with their disciples; Te—they all; Sa-Sahitah—with the fire; Pratyaghrhan—were properly welcomed; Samutthayah—standing; Sa-Sankrithaah—being gathered; Tasya—His; Tejasa—with splendor.\n.\n\nLord Vamandev, wearing the Munj's Mekhla, the sacred thread, the deer skin as an upper garment, and the matted hair,\nentered that Yagya hall as a Brahmin boy. Their radiance diminished all the priests and\ntheir disciples; they rose from their respective seats and, bowing,\neveryone welcomed them in a proper manner. The host is delightful, beautiful to see. The embodied form, therefore, he removes the seat. 26. Meaning of the word:\n\nYajamanah - King, who had all the priests engaged in performing the Yagya (sacrificial ritual); Pramuditah - very pleased;\nDarshaniyam - pleasing to behold; Manoramam - so beautiful; Roopam - endowed with beauty; Anurupam - equal to their physical beauty;\nAvayavam - various parts of the body; Tasmai - to them; Asanam - place to sit; Aharat - given..\n\nSeeing Lord Vamandev, Bali Maharaj, with great joy, with great pleasure, provided him with a seat to sit. The beautiful parts of God's body were contributing to the beauty of his body.\nThey were contributing.\nSalutations to the lotus feet of the Lord. Avanijyarchayamas muktasangamanoramam. 27."
