      "textEn": "390\n\nGod did this to show his power. For other desires, for the king, for the king of the world. Nanha prapnoti vai vidyaavadarthapratigraha. 17. Meaning of the word\n\nNa—No; Anyat—Something else; Te—From you; Kamaye—I beg; Rajan—Oh King; Vadanyat—Those who are so charitable; Jagat—The world; Ishwarat—From the king of all the universe; Na—No; Enah—Sorrow; Prapnoti—Is obtained; Vai—Indeed; Vidvān—The wise;\nYavat—As much as one needs; Pratigrahaḥ—Taking donations from others. Oh King of the Universe! Although you are very generous and can give me as much land as I want,\nyou can give it to me, but I do not want to ask you for unnecessary things. If a scholar\nA Brahmin takes donations from others according to his needs, then he does not get caught in sinful deeds. Meaning: A Brahmin or a monk can ask others for donations, but if he takes more than necessary,\nhe is liable to punishment. No person can consume more of God's wealth than necessary.\nLord Vamandev was indirectly indicating to Bali Maharaj that he\nhas more land than he needs. In the physical world, all suffering is due to waste. Man\nCollects wealth blindly and also wastes it. Such activities are sinful. All property belongs to God, and all living beings, being children of God, are entitled to use their supreme father's property,\nbut no one can take more than what is necessary. This\nprinciple should be followed by Brahmins or monks in particular because they thrive on the strength of others. Thus Vamandev was an ideal worshipper as he only asked for Three-legged land. Undoubtedly, there is a difference between their feet and the feet of an ordinary person. God, with his inconceivable\nmight, can encompass the entire universe with an infinite measure, which includes the upper, lower, and\nmiddle realms. Sribaliruvacha\nOh Brahmins, you are the elders with the consent of the elders.\nYou are childish, full of childishness, selfish, and ignorant. 1. Lexicon"
